{"name":"Osage Orange","hex":"#f4a045","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"osage orange","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Osage orange."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/osage_orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maclura pomifera","blocks":[{"text":"Maclura pomifera, commonly known as the Osage orange, is a small deciduous tree or large shrub, native to the south-central United States. It is a member of the mulberry family, Moraceae. It typically grows about 8 to 15 m (30–50 ft) tall. The distinctive multiple fruit resembles an immature orange, is roughly spherical, bumpy, 8 to 15 cm (3–6 in) in diameter, and turns bright yellow-green in the fall. The fruit excretes a sticky white latex when cut or damaged. Despite the name \"Osage orange\", it is not related to the orange."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Maclura_pomifera2.jpg/330px-Maclura_pomifera2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maclura_pomifera","wikidata":"Q1066106"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Maclura_pomifera_001.JPG/250px-Maclura_pomifera_001.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_con